  - [1mCaller passes a client-generated tracking ID[0m ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m in the submit handler, threaded into [38;5;153mlogin[39m). Buys
    you a correlation key for this login attempt that works today with no backend. Costs: it identifies the [3mattempt[0m, not
    the person — it's not a stable user identity, so it won't answer "who logged in" across sessions. Cheap to change
    later.
  - [1mCaller passes a real user identifier the app already knows[0m (e.g. a tenant/account ID from elsewhere in the app).
    Buys you genuine identity in the signature. Costs: nothing in this repo supplies one, so it means adding a form
    field or another source first — the parameter would be [38;5;153mundefined[39m at the only call site until then. Cheap now,
    awkward to undo once callers depend on it.
  - [1mDon't add a parameter — return the id from [38;5;153mlogin[0m (the stub already returns [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m; add [38;5;153muserId[39m to the
    result, sourced from the API response in the real app). Buys you the actual "who logged in" answer, since the server
    is what knows the user's ID after authenticating. Costs: it's not the change you asked for, and it does nothing
    until the real POST exists. This is the shape that's expensive to walk back later, because it's the one other code
    would read.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m A [38;5;153muserId[39m [3mparameter[0m on [38;5;153mlogin[39m asks the client to already know the identity that logging in
  is supposed to establish — the caller can only ever pass the username it just typed, or a made-up ID. If the goal is
  tracking who logged in, the identity belongs on the way out, not the way in. That said, if you have a caller in mind
  that genuinely holds a user ID, the first or second option is real and I'll do it.

  Also worth flagging regardless of choice: line 5 [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms the username on every attempt, and adding an ID there
  widens what lands in browser logs.
